<div id="export-panel" style="display: none;">
  <div class="oscar-export">
    <p class="oscar-export-lead">
      Publishing turns your interface into a page of its own, separate from
      the editor, that OSCAR serves at an address any phone, tablet or
      computer on the network can open. It stays as it is while you carry on
      editing, until you publish it again.
    </p>

    <!-- The misunderstanding behind every "my page does nothing" report,
         stated before the button rather than after it. -->
    <div class="oscar-export-warning">
      <strong>OSCAR has to keep running.</strong>
      A browser cannot send OSC, DMX or MIDI on its own, so the page hands
      everything to OSCAR, which puts it on the network. The device showing
      the page has to be on the same network as this computer.
    </div>

    <div id="export-pages" class="oscar-export-warning" style="display: none;">
      <strong>Only the first page is published.</strong>
      This project has <span id="export-page-count"></span> pages, and a
      published page is a single surface.
    </div>

    <div class="oscar-export-field">
      <label for="export-name">Name</label>
      <input id="export-name" class="o-input" type="text" placeholder="my-interface" />
    </div>

    <div id="export-error" class="oscar-export-error" style="display: none;"></div>
    <div id="export-note" class="oscar-export-note" style="display: none;"></div>

    <button id="publish-button" class="o-btn o-btn-primary oscar-export-go" type="button">
      Publish on this OSCAR
    </button>

    <div id="publish-result" class="oscar-publish-result" style="display: none;">
      <div id="publish-qr" class="oscar-publish-qr" role="img" aria-label="QR code for the address"></div>
      <div class="oscar-publish-where">
        <span id="publish-status" class="oscar-publish-status"></span>
        <a id="publish-link" class="o-link oscar-publish-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener"></a>
        <span class="oscar-export-hint">Scan it with the phone's camera, or type the address.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="published-box" class="oscar-published" style="display: none;">
      <div class="oscar-published-title">Published on this OSCAR</div>
      <ul id="published-list" class="oscar-published-list"></ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Closed by default. Publishing needs none of this: a published page
         finds OSCAR by the address it was opened at. Only a file that leaves
         OSCAR has to be told where OSCAR is, so the two fields live here,
         with the one button that uses them. -->
    <details id="export-advanced" class="oscar-export-advanced">
      <summary>Advanced: download the page as a file</summary>

      <p class="oscar-export-hint">
        The same page as an <code>.html</code> file, to host on a web server
        or open on another computer. It does not work opened from a phone's
        or tablet's own storage; publish for those.
      </p>

      <div class="oscar-export-row">
        <div class="oscar-export-field">
          <label for="export-host">OSCAR is reachable at</label>
          <input id="export-host" class="o-input" type="text" spellcheck="false" />
        </div>
        <div class="oscar-export-field">
          <label for="export-port">Bridge port</label>
          <input id="export-port" class="o-input" type="number" min="1" max="65535" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="oscar-export-hint">
        A file cannot ask where OSCAR is, so it is told here. Both are filled
        in with what this OSCAR reports for itself. Use the address other
        devices see, not <code>localhost</code>, unless the file will only
        ever be opened on this computer. If OSCAR moves later there is no need
        to download again: add
        <code>?oscar-host=ADDRESS&amp;oscar-port=PORT</code> to the page's
        address.
      </p>

      <button id="export-button" class="o-btn oscar-export-go" type="button">Download the file</button>
    </details>
  </div>
</div>
